            <ArticleTitle>Live births, cesarean sections and the development of menstrual abnormalities.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>111-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the relationship between number of livebirths and cesarean sections on the development of menstrual abnormalities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The effect of number of livebirths and cesarean sections on menstrual abnormalities in the late reproductive period was evaluated using data from the Harvard Study of Moods and Cycles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 298 women with no livebirths and 606 women with livebirths of which 184 had a history of cesarean section. Among all women, increasing number of livebirths was associated with an increasing trend in reported heavy menses, prolonged flow &gt;6 days, and a decreasing trend in reported dysmenorrhea. This effect was amplified among women with a history of cesarean section.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Increasing number of livebirths is associated with heavy and prolonged menstrual flow during the late reproductive period. This association is particularly stronger in women with a history of cesarean section.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
